        <div id="post-952" class="post-952 post type-post status-publish format-standard hentry category-agile category-coaching category-scrum">

          <div class="post-header">
           <h3 class="post-title"><a href="http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/2012/01/time-box-a-holistic-view-on-sprints-and-iterations/" rel="bookmark" title="Permanent Link: Time box: A Holistic View on Sprints and Iterations">Time box: A Holistic View on Sprints and Iterations</a></h3>
           <p class="post-date">
            <span class="month">Jan</span>
            <span class="day">16</span>
           </p>
           <p class="post-author">
            <span class="info">Posted by <a href="http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/author/cromwellryan/" title="Posts by cromwellryan ">cromwellryan</a> in <a href="http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/category/agile/" title="View all posts in Agile" rel="category tag">Agile</a>, <a href="http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/category/coaching/" title="View all posts in Coaching" rel="category tag">Coaching</a>, <a href="http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/category/scrum/" title="View all posts in Scrum" rel="category tag">Scrum</a> | <a href="http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/2012/01/time-box-a-holistic-view-on-sprints-and-iterations/#respond" class="comments"  title="Comment on Time box: A Holistic View on Sprints and Iterations"><span class="dsq-postid" rel="952 http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/?p=952">No Comments</span></a>              </span>
           </p>
          </div>

          <div class="post-content clearfix">
          <p>Over the last few years we’ve seen a growing discontent around the idea of Iterations or Sprints.&#160; It excites me that the software community is actively engaged in questioning the long held canon of Agile practices.&#160; Through these explorations we as an industry will find a greater depth of understanding.&#160; Over time this can only [...]</p>

                    </div>
        </div>
        <!-- /post -->
        
        <!-- post -->
        <div id="post-947" class="post-947 post type-post status-publish format-standard hentry category-agile category-coaching category-craftsmanship category-scrum">

          <div class="post-header">
           <h3 class="post-title"><a href="http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/2012/01/definition-of-done-discovery/" rel="bookmark" title="Permanent Link: Definition of Done Discovery">Definition of Done Discovery</a></h3>
           <p class="post-date">
            <span class="month">Jan</span>
            <span class="day">4</span>
           </p>
           <p class="post-author">
            <span class="info">Posted by <a href="http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/author/cromwellryan/" title="Posts by cromwellryan ">cromwellryan</a> in <a href="http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/category/agile/" title="View all posts in Agile" rel="category tag">Agile</a>, <a href="http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/category/coaching/" title="View all posts in Coaching" rel="category tag">Coaching</a>, <a href="http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/category/craftsmanship/" title="View all posts in Craftsmanship" rel="category tag">Craftsmanship</a>, <a href="http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/category/scrum/" title="View all posts in Scrum" rel="category tag">Scrum</a> | <a href="http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/2012/01/definition-of-done-discovery/#respond" class="comments"  title="Comment on Definition of Done Discovery"><span class="dsq-postid" rel="947 http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/2012/01/definition-of-done-discovery/">No Comments</span></a>              </span>
           </p>
          </div>

          <div class="post-content clearfix">
          <p>One of the important aspects of Scrum and the more fundamental concept incremental delivery is building Done software each iteration.&#160; There are a lot of holes in that statement, but that makes sense; Scrum is full of holes… on purpose.&#160; If you want answers rather than a framework built for learning, Agile ain’t for you. [...]</p>

                    </div>
        </div>
        <!-- /post -->
        
        <!-- post -->
        <div id="post-944" class="post-944 post type-post status-publish format-standard hentry category-net category-agile category-tdd category-testing">

          <div class="post-header">
           <h3 class="post-title"><a href="http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/2012/01/your-test-suite-sucks-do-it-again/" rel="bookmark" title="Permanent Link: Your Test Suite Sucks&hellip; do it again">Your Test Suite Sucks&hellip; do it again</a></h3>
           <p class="post-date">
            <span class="month">Jan</span>
            <span class="day">4</span>
           </p>
           <p class="post-author">
            <span class="info">Posted by <a href="http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/author/cromwellryan/" title="Posts by cromwellryan ">cromwellryan</a> in <a href="http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/category/net/" title="View all posts in .Net" rel="category tag">.Net</a>, <a href="http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/category/agile/" title="View all posts in Agile" rel="category tag">Agile</a>, <a href="http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/category/tdd/" title="View all posts in TDD" rel="category tag">TDD</a>, <a href="http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/category/testing/" title="View all posts in Testing" rel="category tag">Testing</a> | <a href="http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/2012/01/your-test-suite-sucks-do-it-again/#comments" class="comments"  title="Comment on Your Test Suite Sucks&hellip; do it again"><span class="dsq-postid" rel="944 http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/2012/01/your-test-suite-sucks-do-it-again/">1 Comment</span></a>              </span>
           </p>
          </div>

          <div class="post-content clearfix">
          <p>A pretty smart dude I worked with once told this story of a team he was on.&#160; They spent a few days, maybe even a week, deploying their app.&#160; It was painful, obviously.&#160; They’re response: “That sucked.&#160; Let’s do it again tomorrow.” Most people try to avoid hard things or at least delay them until [...]</p>

                    </div>
        </div>
